## Proof

Let $K$ be an arbitrary field, and let $\mathfrak{gl}(2,K) = M_2(K)$ denote the Lie algebra of $2\times 2$ matrices over $K$ with bracket $[A,B]=AB-BA$. The **center** is
$$Z(\mathfrak{gl}(2,K)) = \{A \in \mathfrak{gl}(2,K) : [A,B]=0 \text{ for all } B\in\mathfrak{gl}(2,K)\}.$$

### Step 1. Scalar matrices lie in the center.

For any $\lambda \in K$, the matrix $\lambda I = \begin{pmatrix}\lambda&0\\0&\lambda\end{pmatrix}$ satisfies
$$[\lambda I, B] = \lambda IB - B\lambda I = \lambda B - \lambda B = 0$$
for every $B\in M_2(K)$, since $\lambda$ is a scalar and commutes with every entry of $B$. Hence $\{\lambda I : \lambda\in K\}\subseteq Z(\mathfrak{gl}(2,K))$.

### Step 2. Every central element is scalar.

Let $A = \begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}\in Z(\mathfrak{gl}(2,K))$. We use the standard basis
$$E_{11}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad E_{12}=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad E_{21}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad E_{22}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.$$

**Commuting with $E_{12}$:** A direct computation gives
$$[A,E_{12}] = AE_{12}-E_{12}A = \begin{pmatrix}-b & a-d \\ 0 & b\end{pmatrix}.$$
Setting this to zero yields $b=0$ and $a=d$.

**Commuting with $E_{21}$:** With $b=0$ and $a=d$, write $A = \begin{pmatrix}a&0\\c&a\end{pmatrix}$. Then
$$[A,E_{21}] = AE_{21}-E_{21}A = \begin{pmatrix}c&0\\0&-c\end{pmatrix}.$$
Setting this to zero yields $c=0$.

Therefore $A = \begin{pmatrix}a&0\\0&a\end{pmatrix} = aI$ for some $a\in K$.

### Conclusion

Combining Steps 1 and 2,
$$\boxed{Z(\mathfrak{gl}(2,K)) = \{\lambda I_2 : \lambda \in K\} \cong K,}$$
i.e. the center of $\mathfrak{gl}(2,K)$ is the one-dimensional subspace of scalar matrices, isomorphic (as a Lie algebra with trivial bracket) to the additive group of $K$.

